## Proposed successor to decision 0001

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) remains unchanged as
a historical record. This proposal replaces subject matching as sufficient evidence for a merge.
The support desk must approve the policy; this document does not claim that approval.

## Context

The [incident review](/kb/incidents/4471-review) shows that matching subjects can hide different
failures, and that different subjects or intake tags can describe the same failure.
The existing tenant-based classifier also assigns some conflicting bodies to a broad bucket;
therefore its label alone is not proof of diagnosis.

## Proposed rule

1. Deduplicate overlapping export rows by report ID, not by subject, body, or person.
2. Read the body and record the product, tenant, locale, form version, channel, and reproduction.
3. Use subject matching to find candidate tickets only. Merge when the observed failure and
   reproduction support the same mechanism and fix boundary.
4. Keep different mechanisms in separate tickets, with distinct report counts and ID coverage.
5. Keep unknown or conflicting evidence unconfirmed and visible for investigation.
6. Reconcile new reports against existing IDs and review changed copies before updating counts.

## Limits and verification

The scheduling classifier models shared-parser and booking-v3-hint failures for known
day-first tenants. It is not a general classifier for other support products.
Preserve symptom exceptions as unresolved until reproduced, even when the tenant classifier
assigns them to an existing group. A fix must cover the actual failure, not merely its intake label.

Before adopting this proposal, replay the explorer fixtures, review the exceptions, and ask
the support desk to approve the operating rule. Reconsider the rule when fresh evidence changes
the failure mechanisms, not only when subject wording changes.

